sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-07> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Skeletal elements comparable in size to a single cell (sponge spicules, radiolarian and foraminiferal skeletons) are shaped by molecular forces such as surface tension, whereas massive skeletons such as vertebrate bones are shaped by gravity, muscle action and mechanical loads, so scale produces essential differences in their controlling forces and forms.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Cell-sized skeletal elements are shaped by molecular forces, large ones by mechanics";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IX, “On Concretions, Spicules, and Spicular Skeletons”; topic “Classification of skeletal forms by scale”. Scope: skeletal elements of all sizes, from protist skeletons and spicules to vertebrate bones"
}
